        <label><input type="radio" name="fee-mode" value="weekly"> Flat weekly fee</label>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div data-mode-percent class="calculator-panel field-span" style="padding:0;border:0;background:transparent;box-shadow:none;">
      <div>
        <label for="fee-linehaul">Linehaul (dollars)</label>
        <input id="fee-linehaul" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.01" value="2000">
        <p class="field-hint">Example: 2,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="fee-fsc">Fuel surcharge (dollars)</label>
        <input id="fee-fsc" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.01" value="500">
        <p class="field-hint">Example: 500. Use 0 if the confirmation has no fuel surcharge.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="fee-percent">Dispatch fee (percent)</label>
        <input id="fee-percent" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="99.99" step="0.1" value="8">
        <p class="field-hint">Example: 8. This is not a typical or recommended rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="field-span choice-group">
        <p id="fee-basis-label">Charge the percentage on</p>
        <div class="choice-row" role="radiogroup" aria-labelledby="fee-basis-label">
          <label><input type="radio" name="fee-basis" value="gross" checked> Gross (linehaul + fuel surcharge)</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="fee-basis" value="linehaul"> Linehaul only</label>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div data-mode-weekly hidden class="calculator-panel field-span" style="padding:0;border:0;background:transparent;box-shadow:none;">
      <div>
        <label for="fee-weekly">Weekly dispatch fee (dollars)</label>
        <input id="fee-weekly" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.01" value="400">
        <p class="field-hint">Example: 400 per week per truck. Not a quoted rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="fee-weekly-gross">Weekly gross (dollars)</label>
        <input id="fee-weekly-gross" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="0.01" value="8000">
        <p class="field-hint">Example: 8,000 in gross for that truck that week.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="calculator-actions">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-calculate>Calculate</button>
    </div>
    <div class="calculator-result" data-result aria-live="polite">
      <h3>Estimate</h3>
      <p class="calculator-error" data-error></p>
      <div class="result-grid">
        <div>
          <p>Fee is calculated on</p>
          <strong data-basis></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Dispatch fee</p>
          <strong data-fee></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>What is left</p>
          <strong data-keep></strong>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Break-even raise</p>
          <strong data-breakeven></strong>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="calculator-note">This is an estimate from the numbers you entered. It is not a quote from a dispatch service and not a statement of what you should pay or charge.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<article class="prose">
  <h2 id="percentage-or-weekly">Percentage or weekly</h2>
  <p>Independent dispatch services in the United States commonly charge a percentage of each load, a flat weekly or monthly fee per truck, or a mix of the two. There is no official standard rate. The agreement should also say what the percentage is charged on, and whether cancelled or unpaid loads still carry a fee.</p>
  <p>The starting figures on this page — a $2,000 linehaul, a $500 fuel surcharge, and an 8% fee — are examples so the arithmetic is visible when you arrive. They are not a market survey. If your agreement uses a weekly fee, switch the calculator and enter that week’s fee and gross instead.</p>
  <p>For the job itself, and for how pay is usually written into a first carrier agreement, see <a href="/getting-started/how-to-become-a-truck-dispatcher-with-no-experience/">how to become a truck dispatcher with no experience</a>. The <a href="/owner-operators/">owner-operator shelf</a> covers the carrier side of that relationship.</p>

  <h2 id="the-break-even-raise">The break-even raise</h2>
  <p>A percentage fee reduces what the carrier keeps unless the booked amount rises enough to cover it. If the fee is 8% of the figures it is charged on, those figures have to rise by 8 divided by 92, or about 8.7%, before the carrier keeps the same money as they would have without the fee. The calculator performs that step with the percentage you enter.</p>
  <p>That is not a promise that a dispatcher will obtain that raise. It is only the arithmetic of the fee. Whether the service is worth it still depends on the rates booked, the empty miles avoided, and the office work that is included. For the per-mile view of a single load, use the <a href="/tools/true-rate-per-mile/">true rate per mile calculator</a>.</p>
</article>

